---
import Math from './Math.astro';
import {pairs,lowSpinState,singletProbabilities} from '../lib/quantum.mjs';
const initialProbabilities=singletProbabilities(lowSpinState(globalThis.Math.PI/2,0));
---
<div class="exhibit coherence-lab" data-coherence-lab><div class="exhibit-heading"><span class="eyebrow">Figure 03 / Relative phase at fixed total spin</span><span class="exhibit-tag">Four qubits · J = 0</span></div><p class="coherence-purpose">This figure asks whether total spin determines what a pair measurement will do. It does not. We compare superpositions of two orthogonal four-qubit states, both with total spin zero.</p><div class="coherence-basis"><article><h4><Math tex={"|e_0\\rangle"}/> · two singlets</h4><Math display tex={"|e_0\\rangle=|s\\rangle_{12}|s\\rangle_{34}"}/><p>Pairs 1–2 and 3–4 each have spin 0.</p></article><article><h4><Math tex={"|e_1\\rangle"}/> · two triplets, coupled to spin 0</h4><Math display tex={"|e_1\\rangle=\\frac{|t_+t_-\\rangle-|t_0t_0\\rangle+|t_-t_+\\rangle}{\\sqrt3}"}/><p>Each pair has spin 1, but the two pairs jointly have spin 0. Here <Math tex={"|t_+\\rangle=|00\\rangle"}/>, <Math tex={"|t_0\\rangle=(|01\\rangle+|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2"}/>, and <Math tex={"|t_-\\rangle=|11\\rangle"}/> denote the triplet kets; the first and second factors refer to 1–2 and 3–4.</p></article></div><div class="coherence-formula"><Math display tex={"|\\psi_\\varphi\\rangle=\\frac{|e_0\\rangle+e^{i\\varphi}|e_1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}"}/></div><div class="coherence-grid"><div><h3>Change only the relative phase</h3><p class="small-copy">Both components have probability ½. Moving the slider changes their interference, not their weights. Watch the cross-pair bars: 1–3 and 1–4 exchange their singlet probabilities between φ = 0 and φ = π.</p><p class="fine-print">This slider selects a mathematical state; it does not implement an STP gate.</p><label class="control-label" for="coherence-phase">Relative phase φ <output data-phase-output>0</output></label><input id="coherence-phase" type="range" min="0" max="360" step="1" value="0"/><div class="range-labels"><span>0</span><span>π</span><span>2π</span></div><div class="preset-row"><button data-phase-preset="0">φ = 0</button><button data-phase-preset="90">φ = π/2</button><button data-phase-preset="180">φ = π</button></div><button class="quiet-button" data-incoherent aria-pressed="false">Compare with an incoherent mixture</button><p class="coherence-status" role="status">Coherent superposition · equal amplitudes</p></div><div class="coherence-plot"><div class="eyebrow">Probability of a singlet outcome</div><div data-coherence-bars>{initialProbabilities.map((p,i)=><div class="prob-bar"><span>{pairs[i].map(x=>x+1).join('–')}</span><div><i style={`width:${p*100}%`}/></div><strong>{globalThis.Math.round(p*1000)/10}%</strong></div>)}</div><p class="coherence-readout" data-coherence-explanation aria-live="polite"></p><div class="invariant-row"><span>Total spin <strong>0</strong></span><span>Sum over pairs <strong>3</strong></span></div></div></div><details class="exhibit-details"><summary>Which coherence? Which resource?</summary><div class="details-body"><Math display tex={String.raw`|\psi_\varphi\rangle=\frac{|e_0\rangle+e^{i\varphi}|e_1\rangle}{\sqrt{2}}`}/><p>The two states above form an orthonormal basis of the four-qubit J = 0 multiplicity space. The mixture is <Math tex={String.raw`\tfrac12|e_0\rangle\langle e_0|+\tfrac12|e_1\rangle\langle e_1|`}/>. Both remain in the same spin sector.</p><Math display tex={String.raw`\sum_{i<j}P_s^{(ij)}=\frac{n(n+2)}8 I-\frac12\mathbf J^2`}/><p>Thus total singlet weight is fixed by total spin. This is why a resource theory must look beyond that single number. This figure studies coherence within a sector; it does not assume free access to coherence between different global-spin sectors.</p><p><strong>The phase slider compares mathematical states; it is not an STP phase gate.</strong> Starting from singlets or maximally mixed input, bare STP has real projectors and preserves real density matrices. At φ = π/2 or 3π/2, all six probabilities are ½, just as for the mixture. Even adaptive bare-STP sequences cannot detect that imaginary coherence: their measurement effects remain real.</p></div></details></div>
<script>import {initCoherenceLabs} from '../scripts/exhibits.mjs';initCoherenceLabs();</script>
